{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01CwcjRDA4NvbrBTwEwhQKMg","type":"tool_result","content":"1\t# Draft Reference Gap Memo\n2\t\n3\t**Status:** ready — three targeted retrieval needs.\n4\t\n5\tExisting coverage is adequate for design-based estimation (Aronow, Ugander, HT variants), GATE designs, and regression-based GATE estimation (Chin, Han and Ugander). Quantitative claims are grounded in `paper_data.json` and are not flagged. The gaps below are the places where positioning or background statements are thinly supported.\n6\t\n7\t## Missing literatures\n8\t- Exposure-mapping misspecification and unknown interference\n9\t- Foundational interference/spillover and bias of naive estimators\n10\t- Variance and confidence-interval estimation under network dependence\n11\t\n12\t## Targets\n13\t\n14\t1. **thesis-exposure-mapping-misspecification** (introduction, priority HIGH, positioning)\n15\t   The central thesis is that fidelity of the fitted exposure mapping governs accuracy. The corner-extrapolation account in Results supports it. Only Aronow2013EstimatingAC is cited for \"exposure mapping\", and nothing covers misspecified mappings, unknown interference, or extrapolation to the all-treated/all-control corners (overlap/positivity). Reviewers would expect the paper to position itself against that work.\n16\t   - Anchor: \"accuracy is governed by the fidelity of the fitted exposure mapping to the generating mechanism\"\n17\t   - Search topics: misspecified exposure mapping bias under network interference; average treatment effects under unknown interference; positivity/overlap extrapolation for global effects.\n18\t\n19\t2. **intro-interference-dim-bias** (introduction, priority MEDIUM, background)\n20\t   The first paragraph states that network A/B tests violate no-interference and that DiM is systematically biased for the GATE. These are uncited foundational claims; only HT estimators are cited there.\n21\t   - Anchor: \"The difference-in-means (DiM) estimator, which is unbiased without interference, is systematically biased...\"\n22\t   - Search topics: causal inference with interference/spillovers; bias of difference-in-means in network A/B tests.\n23\t\n24\t3. **reliability-interval-undercoverage** (results, reliability subsection, priority MEDIUM, comparator)\n25\t   Coverage of 0.839 against a nominal 0.95 is a headline negative result. The draft explains that HC1 intervals ignore cluster dependence and use no resampling, but it cites no work on variance estimation or confidence intervals under network dependence.\n26\t   - Anchor: \"ignores cluster dependence under the cluster design and uses no resampling\"\n27\t   - Search topics: variance estimation under network dependence in interference experiments; cluster-robust or network HAC standard errors.\n28\t\n29\t## Not flagged\n30\tThe related-work paragraphs on design-based estimation, designs, and regression adjustment are densely cited. The HT, graph-cluster-randomization, and Chin/Han comparators are covered. GNN/Facebook100 mentions appear only in limitations as untested extensions and need no added support.\n31\t"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"3357425c-e6b2-40a3-a1ec-c34bbfe9c538","uuid":"234e7547-d228-4c91-8277-853b670e42e3","timestamp":"2026-11-11T08:30:39.949Z","tool_use_result":{"type":"text","file":{"filePath":"/workspace/paper/draft_reference_gaps.md","content":"# Draft Reference Gap Memo\n\n**Status:** ready — three targeted retrieval needs.\n\nExisting coverage is adequate for design-based estimation (Aronow, Ugander, HT variants), GATE designs, and regression-based GATE estimation (Chin, Han and Ugander). Quantitative claims are grounded in `paper_data.json` and are not flagged. The gaps below are the places where positioning or background statements are thinly supported.\n\n## Missing literatures\n- Exposure-mapping misspecification and unknown interference\n- Foundational interference/spillover and bias of naive estimators\n- Variance and confidence-interval estimation under network dependence\n\n## Targets\n\n1. **thesis-exposure-mapping-misspecification** (introduction, priority HIGH, positioning)\n   The central thesis is that fidelity of the fitted exposure mapping governs accuracy. The corner-extrapolation account in Results supports it. Only Aronow2013EstimatingAC is cited for \"exposure mapping\", and nothing covers misspecified mappings, unknown interference, or extrapolation to the all-treated/all-control corners (overlap/positivity). Reviewers would expect the paper to position itself against that work.\n   - Anchor: \"accuracy is governed by the fidelity of the fitted exposure mapping to the generating mechanism\"\n   - Search topics: misspecified exposure mapping bias under network interference; average treatment effects under unknown interference; positivity/overlap extrapolation for global effects.\n\n2. **intro-interference-dim-bias** (introduction, priority MEDIUM, background)\n   The first paragraph states that network A/B tests violate no-interference and that DiM is systematically biased for the GATE. These are uncited foundational claims; only HT estimators are cited there.\n   - Anchor: \"The difference-in-means (DiM) estimator, which is unbiased without interference, is systematically biased...\"\n   - Search topics: causal inference with interference/spillovers; bias of difference-in-means in network A/B tests.\n\n3. **reliability-interval-undercoverage** (results, reliability subsection, priority MEDIUM, comparator)\n   Coverage of 0.839 against a nominal 0.95 is a headline negative result. The draft explains that HC1 intervals ignore cluster dependence and use no resampling, but it cites no work on variance estimation or confidence intervals under network dependence.\n   - Anchor: \"ignores cluster dependence under the cluster design and uses no resampling\"\n   - Search topics: variance estimation under network dependence in interference experiments; cluster-robust or network HAC standard errors.\n\n## Not flagged\nThe related-work paragraphs on design-based estimation, designs, and regression adjustment are densely cited. The HT, graph-cluster-randomization, and Chin/Han comparators are covered. GNN/Facebook100 mentions appear only in limitations as untested extensions and need no added support.\n","numLines":31,"startLine":1,"totalLines":31}}}
