{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01HSXPGJEhmBe7nrRBqwyPuD","type":"tool_result","content":"41:A/B tests on social networks and marketplaces violate the no-interference assumption: whether a user's outcome changes depends on who else is treated. The platform's decision-relevant estimand is then the \\emph{global average treatment effect} (GATE), the mean over units of the difference between the outcome when all units are treated and when all are untreated. The difference-in-means (DiM) es\n45:\\paragraph{Question and thesis.} We ask: in a single randomized network experiment, how accurately does a regression-based GATE estimator recover the truth, and which properties of the interference mechanism, graph and assignment design determine when it works? Our reading of the evidence is that \\emph{accuracy is governed by the fidelity of the fitted exposure mapping to the generating mechani\n108:\\paragraph{Frozen BIC-gated exposure library (studied estimator).} The studied estimator fits, by OLS, the linear model above plus 14 alternatives, each with few parameters: a treated-neighbour count; a two-hop walk fraction; interactions with log-degree and covariates (three variants); saturating $1-e^{-kf}$ with $k\\in\\{1,3,8\\}$; sigmoids of $f$ centred at $\\{0.25,0.5,0.75\\}$ with scale 0.1; \n227: on the plane mean. The interval is built from HC1 sandwich errors for a fixed library, widened by the departure from the linear estimate; it does not model selection uncertainty beyond that spread, ignores cluster dependence under the cluster design and uses no resampling. We report the undercoverage as an observed outcome; per-case coverage is not recorded, and we do not attribute the shortf\n284:\\paragraph{Fallbacks.} If the linear fit fails or any step raises, the estimator returns linear OLS with an HC1 interval; a constant outcome returns the estimate 0 with a degenerate interval.\n81: title = {Optimized Covariance Design for AB Test on Social Network under Interference},\n102:@misc{Ugander2013GraphCluster,","is_error":false}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"3357425c-e6b2-40a3-a1ec-c34bbfe9c538","uuid":"11855fbb-a996-4c0c-837b-c8e5d815476b","timestamp":"2026-11-11T08:30:44.632Z","tool_use_result":{"stdout":"41:A/B tests on social networks and marketplaces violate the no-interference assumption: whether a user's outcome changes depends on who else is treated. The platform's decision-relevant estimand is then the \\emph{global average treatment effect} (GATE), the mean over units of the difference between the outcome when all units are treated and when all are untreated. The difference-in-means (DiM) es\n45:\\paragraph{Question and thesis.} We ask: in a single randomized network experiment, how accurately does a regression-based GATE estimator recover the truth, and which properties of the interference mechanism, graph and assignment design determine when it works? Our reading of the evidence is that \\emph{accuracy is governed by the fidelity of the fitted exposure mapping to the generating mechani\n108:\\paragraph{Frozen BIC-gated exposure library (studied estimator).} The studied estimator fits, by OLS, the linear model above plus 14 alternatives, each with few parameters: a treated-neighbour count; a two-hop walk fraction; interactions with log-degree and covariates (three variants); saturating $1-e^{-kf}$ with $k\\in\\{1,3,8\\}$; sigmoids of $f$ centred at $\\{0.25,0.5,0.75\\}$ with scale 0.1; \n227: on the plane mean. The interval is built from HC1 sandwich errors for a fixed library, widened by the departure from the linear estimate; it does not model selection uncertainty beyond that spread, ignores cluster dependence under the cluster design and uses no resampling. We report the undercoverage as an observed outcome; per-case coverage is not recorded, and we do not attribute the shortf\n284:\\paragraph{Fallbacks.} If the linear fit fails or any step raises, the estimator returns li